#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""How well does a mask tile, and what does each way of building one cost?

  seamlab <sample>...              build a direct mask several ways, compare
  seamlab --score <mask>...        score finished masks instead
  seamlab --check <sample>         confirm the Python model matches build/dither

This is the bench the `direct` generator's seam handling was settled on. It
exists because that question has a lot of plausible answers and only
measurement separates them -- see the rejected list in CLAUDE.md's `direct`
section, every entry of which was killed here.

Everything is measured on the FIELD, before any ranking. That is deliberate.
Rank-flattening is scale-free: it maps whatever ordering exists onto 0..254
regardless of amplitude, so a low-frequency component of *any* size becomes a
full-scale density modulation in the mask. Looking at the mask therefore tells
you that something is wrong without telling you how much was added or by what.
The field tells you both, and `plain` -- the untouched crop -- is the reference
every row is read against.

  work         edge mismatch over interior difference, before any repair. How
               much there is to repair. Only meaningful on a texture: the
               denominator collapses on an image that is constant along one
               axis, and n/a is printed rather than a number from a zero
               divisor.

  seam c/r     rank-sum z of the wrapping column and row pairs against the
               interior pairs. ~0 is a field that joins. Positive is a hard
               seam -- the wrapping pair differs more than the texture does.
               Negative is a repair that overshot into a soft seam, the
               wrapping pair differing less, which is equally visible and which
               reading these as magnitudes concealed. A one-pixel measure, so
               it sees a discontinuity either way and is blind to a broad band
               of altered texture.

  >64px        where the field's power sits, as percentages. A field fit to be
  16-64        ranked into a threshold mask has nearly all of it in the fine
  <16px        band; anything a treatment adds to the two coarse bands ends up
               as density modulation.

  swing        spread of the field's local mean, in the sample's own grey
  vs plain     levels, and the same relative to `plain`. This is the low
               frequency a treatment leaves behind. Above 1x means it put some
               in; below means it took some out.

Constructions (--variants, comma separated, default all):

  plain     high-pass the whole sample, crop. No repair. test/refmask itself.
  direct    what `dither --mask-method direct --direct-equalise 0` builds:
            high-pass, then the minimum error boundary cut of `quilt` below.
            Nothing filters after the cut.

            It used to be Moisan's periodic + smooth decomposition followed by a
            wrapped high-pass to take the smooth component back out, and that
            last step was not optional -- see `swing`. Both are still here, as
            `moisan` and `hpafter`, because the comparison is the argument.

  equalise  **what `dither --mask-method direct` builds by default**, and what
            --check compares against: each pixel of the *raw* sample replaced
            by its quantile within a Gaussian neighbourhood of four times the
            high-pass's sigma (--equalise), ties shuffled, then the cut. Not quantised, since
            it is already a continuous quantile. Raw rather than high-passed is
            the point: a clipped sample's zeros are one tie group, and any
            subtraction first orders them by the surface -- the shadow voids
            `--rank local` gave on Kodak-4X-1. See maskutil::local_quantile and
            CLAUDE.md's `direct` section. Exact here; the program counts on a
            sigma/3 grid, which reads a few per cent wider.
  moisan    the decomposition alone, without that last step. Kept because it is
            the bare method and because it is what carried the density defect,
            so it is what the shipped construction had to beat on `swing`.
  wraphp    crop first, then high-pass the tile wrap-padded, then Moisan. The
            "let the filter see a torus" idea. Also ends in a bare
            periodisation, so it carries the same density defect as `moisan`. On film the two are
            indistinguishable by every measure tried. On images that are not
            textures the comparison is unsettled and the metrics disagree with
            each other, so look at the tiling rather than the numbers.

            One thing is definite: wrap-padding a sample with a *gradient* leaves
            the gradient behind. It puts the dark end next to the bright end, so
            the blur errs in opposite directions at the two edges and a residual
            tilt survives across the tile -- on linesr.png the column mean runs
            -23.8 to +23.8 where the reflecting high-pass leaves 0.0 to 0.0.
            Film has no gradient, which is why this never shows there.
  hpafter   crop, Moisan, then a wrap-padded high-pass. Filtering after the
            repair, which a reflecting filter would undo.
  mirror    mirror the tile to 2x and take that. Exactly periodic, and the eye
            finds the symmetry instantly -- included so that is visible as a
            number rather than an assertion.
  damped    Moisan with the two axes' boundary data scaled independently, each
            bisected to land the wrap difference on that axis's interior
            difference. Helps some samples, breaks others.
  blend     histogram-preserving blending (Heitz & Neyret 2018) of the tile with
            its own half-shifts, one pass per axis. No global solve, so nothing
            can be injected into the interior -- which is the defect that rules
            out `moisan` and `wraphp` however well they close the seam. `blend`
            high-passes afterwards as `direct` does; `blendraw` is the bare
            blend; `blendpre` high-passes the sample *first*, which is the
            production order and much the best of the three -- nothing may
            filter after the blend, or the sub-level ordering --quantise exists
            to remove comes straight back.
  poly      `direct` with the high-pass replaced by fitting and removing a
            low-order surface. The premise is that a sample for `direct` is
            grain and nothing else, so its only low-frequency content is a
            gradient or vignetting, which six coefficients can describe and
            which no convolution is needed to remove.

            It is safe and it is measurably unnecessary. On a grain crop the
            tone never dominates the grain -- imposing a 0.35-1.41x wedge only
            reaches tone/overall 0.16, because the grain's own sd is 65 against
            the tone's 10 -- so `plain` is already immune and reads void 1.96
            flat or wedged, with `poly` reading the same. Where filtering *is*
            needed, a full frame, the low-frequency content is picture detail
            rather than a surface and no low order fits it: on DemiEE17 `poly`
            manages 12.3 where the high-pass gets 3.0. It works where nothing
            was wrong and not where something is.

            Kept because it cannot do harm, because the regime it is for --
            strong gradient over weak grain -- is reachable in principle, and
            because the negative result is worth being able to reproduce.

            Divided rather than subtracted it was briefly dither's default,
            and is refuted: dividing by the mean assumes grain amplitude is
            proportional to it, and PanatomicX's amplitude is flat while its
            mean falls 47 to 27, so dividing manufactured a 3.1-to-5.2 amplitude
            gradient and a line at every tile seam. `equalise` replaced it.

  quilt     Efros & Freeman's minimum error boundary cut (2001): overlap the
            tile with its own continuation and cut the lowest-cost path through
            the overlap, per axis. Every output pixel comes from exactly one
            place in the sample and nothing is ever mixed, so unlike `blend` it
            keeps `direct`'s whole claim -- that everything true of the grain is
            true of the mask. It measures like it: patchfit 2.6 and 2.3 on the
            two films, both *below* the real-patch floor, where blend costs
            0.3-1.0 sigma for the averaging it does in the band.

            What it needs is somewhere to cut. The path is invisible only where
            the two candidates agree, which stochastic grain offers constantly
            and structured content does not: on linesr, whose lines sit on a
            gradient so the two edges never agree anywhere, it reads 16.2 where
            the blend reads -5.5. It also needs --overlap more sample than the
            tile, which is a real new constraint and not just a bigger number.
"""
